title:: CaosMIDIClock
summary:: MIDI clock master tap: 24 ppqn and transport from CaosBox.
categories:: CaosBox
related:: Classes/CaosMIDI, Classes/CaosMIDIOut, Classes/MIDIOut

description::
Sends MIDI clock (24 ticks per quarter note) on one output and follows
link::Classes/TempoClock::, specifically code::TempoClock.default::. It therefore
respects the CaosBox BPM and the random-time mode.

Ticks are scheduled at absolute beat positions (1/24 beat apart) so the clock
does not accumulate drift; the normal language-side jitter of a few milliseconds
remains. The clock can keep running while the external device is stopped:
code::sendStart::, code::sendStop:: and code::sendContinue:: control its
transport, and link::Classes/CaosBox:: calls them from code::play::,
code::pause:: and code::stop::.

The usual entry point is link::Classes/CaosMIDI#-attachMasterSync::.

classmethods::

method:: new
code::
c = CaosMIDIClock(midiOut);              // TempoClock.default
c = CaosMIDIClock(midiOut, someClock);
::

instancemethods::

method:: out
method:: clock
Read-only output wrapper and clock.

method:: start
method:: stop
Start or stop the clock ticks. code::stop:: does not send a Stop message.
code::tickBeats:: returns the distance between ticks (1/24 beat).

method:: free
Stops the ticks and sends MIDI Stop to the device.

method:: sendStart
method:: sendStop
method:: sendContinue
Send the corresponding system real-time transport message. code::sendStart::
and code::sendContinue:: mark code::hasStarted:: as true; a tap that never sent
one of those does not get a Continue when CaosBox resumes, it gets Start.

method:: hasStarted
True after the first Start or Continue. It lets the transport layer decide
between Start and Continue without leaving an unsynced device stopped.

method:: tickBeats
Distance between clock ticks in beats (code::1 / 24::).
